{%- comment -%}
  Delivery expectation for one variant — the thing that actually changes the
  decision, placed next to where the decision is made.

  Rendered in two places, which is why it is a snippet rather than inline markup:

  1. Inside <variant-selects>, for products with a real choice of variant. The
     chips answer "which sizes can I get"; this answers "when, for the one I
     picked". It needs no entry in product-info.js — updateOptionValues() swaps
     the whole <variant-selects> element, so anything inside it follows the
     variant automatically.
  2. On its own for single-variant products, which have no picker at all and so
     would otherwise show none of this.

  The own/partner difference is spelled out here in customer words — "in our
  shop" vs "from our supplier" — because the chip only has room for the short
  verdict. A location name is never printed.

  Lead-time wording is free text from theme settings and is never inferred. A
  blank setting, or the degraded `available` state, leaves the status standing
  alone: the shop promises nothing it has not promised.

  Accepts:
  - variant: {Object} variant object

  Usage:
  {%- render 'variant-delivery-line', variant: product.selected_or_first_available_variant -%}
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  capture vdl_state
    render 'variant-stock-state', variant: variant
  endcapture
  assign vdl_state = vdl_state | strip

  assign vdl_lead = ''
  case vdl_state
    when 'own'
      assign vdl_status = 'products.product.stock_state.detail_own' | t
      assign vdl_lead = settings.stock_lead_own | default: ''
    when 'available'
      assign vdl_status = 'products.product.inventory_in_stock' | t
    when 'partner'
      assign vdl_status = 'products.product.stock_state.detail_partner' | t
      assign vdl_lead = settings.stock_lead_partner | default: ''
    else
      assign vdl_status = 'products.product.sold_out' | t
  endcase
  assign vdl_lead = vdl_lead | strip
-%}
{%- comment -%}
  The label is what stops this reading as more of the "Taxes included…" small
  print. On a single-variant product there are no chips above it, so without a
  label it is one unanchored sentence floating between the price block and the
  quantity stepper. With it, the info column reads as a consistent stack of
  labelled fields: SIZE / DELIVERY / QUANTITY.
{%- endcomment -%}
<p class="variant-delivery variant-delivery--{{ vdl_state }}">
  <span class="variant-delivery__label">{{ 'products.product.stock_state.label' | t }}</span>
  <span class="variant-delivery__status">{{ vdl_status }}</span>
  {%- if vdl_lead != blank -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      A real element rather than a ::before on the lead. Generated content is not
      part of the text layer, so the two halves ran together as "From our
      supplierShips in 5-7 working days" for anything reading the DOM.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <span class="variant-delivery__sep" aria-hidden="true">—</span>
    <span class="variant-delivery__lead">{{ vdl_lead }}</span>
  {%- endif -%}
</p>
